  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 AM CDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nToday: Storm Potential\n\nInvigorating upper level northwest flow is driving storm development \nupstream through central Minnesota overnight, focused along an \nenhanced area of low level convergence on Central to Northern Plains \nVWPs. A meager open perturbation well to the north over Lake \nSuperior on GOES derived upper level wind plots has prevented storms \nspreading as far south as the local forecast area as subsequent \nsupplementary forcing from the low level frontal boundary trails \nwell behind, struggling to traverse the Missouri River Valley into \nwest-central Minnesota. Eventual synoptic assistance from an \nadditional perturbation crossing the International Border from \nSaskatchewan into Northern Dakota will drive said frontal boundary \nsoutheast through the Upper Mississippi River Valley.\n\nAhead of a cold front that will push through the region this morning \nand afternoon, a few weak perturbations and a slight increase in the \nnocturnal LLJ will help to initiate some storms, as recent CAMs have \nsupported, during the early morning hours. With limited instability \nand minimal shear and mid-level subsidence, these showers and storms \nshould remain below severe limits, however small hail and gusty \nwinds will be possible. Heading into the mid morning to early \nafternoon timeframe, the cold front will begin to enter our CWA. \nDependent on diurnal timing and southeastern extent of this low \nlevel frontal boundary across the forecast area, these prefrontal \nstorms will likely continue for some of the local forecast area \nprimarily from northeast Iowa into southwest Wisconsin. While \ninstability is expected to be ample, around 2000 to 2500 J/kg of \nMLCAPE, there will be minimal shear present. With the increase in \ninstability though and the fact that any storms that due form along \nand just ahead of the cold front will be in their developing phase, \na storm or two could have severe hail up to 1\". The better severe \ningredients remain in southeast Wisconsin and northern Illinois and \nback west into Nebraska and southern Iowa. All precipitation chances \ndiminish by the early afternoon. Total rainfall from these storms \ncould be over 1\" in some locations as the 00Z HREF LPMM has some \nspots of over 1\" along the I-94 corridor and in far southwest \nWisconsin.\n\nSaturday - Midweek: Dry Early, Storms Return Tuesday\n\nNorthwest flow returns to the region as a trough amplifies to our \neast and the ridge sets up to our west. This ridge decays as it \nmoves eastward into early next week. With our region in between \nthese two features, we look to be mostly dry and slightly cooler \nthrough the weekend until the ridge breaks down on Tuesday. High \ntemperatures stay in the low 70s to low 80s from Saturday through \nMonday. Storm chances return Tuesday as a trough moves down from \nnorthern Minnesota. The latest GEFS and EPS have a widespread 60 to \n90% chance for measurable storms. The exact timing and placement \ndetails of this rain will be hammered out as we get closer to the \nevent.",
